7592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186304063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186303672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe78fafd-1fb0-5c2f-9ba1-e3c0b222dbf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466850 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466851 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nI. $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nIII. $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{18}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "161d56e1-91dc-549e-9bfa-2ed40eaef4a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305366 #edb/ref 1080 "0.008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17647057-986e-5b9c-a8af-af759b573b59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305392 #edb/ref 1080 "images/e7/e7a9385b508c7bb1591501fd8bc830faa78ebc3a2f2ad8279ecfe88328ce8bbd.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305392 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a058e89-acfe-528d-ab94-fd31f2e16a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305556 #edb/ref 1080 "images/b8/b